catalog_position|harness_id|signature
1|rasa|A single dialogue S1 selects actions through a deterministic policy ensemble; ticket locks, fallbacks and handoff paths constrain that operation without supplying an autonomous metasystem.
2|botpress|One code-generating LLMz loop can orchestrate many typed tools, while sandboxing, traces and lifecycle hooks remain parent/runtime support rather than agent-owned metasystem functions.
3|n8n|An AI Agent can delegate to Agent Tool children, use fallback models and pass configured Guardrails, but these remain task decomposition and deterministic support around S1.
4|khoj|A configured personal agent combines conversation memory, private knowledge, web information and tools; its persona and capabilities remain one S1 with parent-authored identity.
5|agno|Tasks mode gives an autonomous team leader a shared task board with ownership, dependencies, status, retry and reassignment controls, establishing agent-owned S2 coordination and S3 current regulation.
6|langchain|The core framework supplies tool-using agents while explicitly outsourcing advanced orchestration to LangGraph, keeping the assessed boundary at S1.
7|llama-index|LlamaIndex ships handoff, agent-as-tool orchestrator and DIY planner patterns; each is operational routing/decomposition rather than first-party S2 mutual adjustment.
8|langflow|Langflow can call remote A2A agents and compose agent components in authored graphs, but those paths remain RPC/delegation and deterministic routing rather than S2.
9|semantic-kernel|A dedicated GroupChatManager exposes next-agent, termination and result-selection decision points as a composable S2 path, while the autonomous coordination policy remains application-supplied.
10|autogpt|A goal-driven block graph runs on schedules/triggers, while its dream pass consolidates internal memory; neither deterministic graph control nor learning-like memory maintenance establishes S4.
11|camel-workforce|Workforce dependency gates provide constructor-owned S2, while a model-driven Coordinator owns whole-workforce assignments, failure response and dynamic capacity as S3; ordinary evaluation and human controls do not close S3*/S5.
12|flowise|Agentflow combines tool-using Agent nodes with model-driven scenario routing, iteration and human-input nodes; these are operational and graph-control primitives rather than S2-S5 organizational functions.
13|dify|Agent v2 runs inside an authored workflow with binding, output checks, failure handling and HITL; those controls remain node/runtime support around S1 rather than an autonomous metasystem.
14|agentverse|Simulation rules regulate turn order, visibility and message interaction among multiple role agents, yielding composable S2; task manager/evaluator stages remain production control rather than S3 or S3*.
15|aider|One coding S1 can split architect and editor roles and automatically lint/test/fix changes; the split and checks remain internal operational feedback rather than metasystem functions.
16|gpt-researcher|A ChiefEditor graph sequences research roles and bounded fact-check revisions; fan-out/fan-in and mandatory same-state QA do not establish S2, S3 or independent S3*.
17|vercel-ai|ToolLoopAgent is one explicit model-tool-result S1; prepare, stop, approval, provider and callback controls remain parent/runtime-owned, leaving no supplied autonomous metasystem function.
18|continue|One workspace coding S1 loops over model-selected tools under runtime/user permission modes; those policies constrain operation rather than regulating a multi-S1 organization.
19|anythingllm|Workspace agents can invoke builder-authored Agent Flows of API, LLM and web steps, but deterministic flow composition expands S1 procedure without establishing S2-S5.
20|metagpt|Default MGX TeamLeader routing is not S2 by itself; its whole-team current plan, progress feedback and authority to close, reset and replace commitments establish agent-owned S3.
21|open-interpreter|Opt-in MultiAgentV2 exposes ongoing team communication as composable S2, while a distinct Guardian approval-review session with deny/interrupt authority exposes composable S3*.
22|langgraph|Low-level graph durability, interrupts and state are S1/runtime infrastructure; no reviewed metasystemic decision right is owned at the harness boundary.
23|autogen-agentchat|Magentic-One's loop/stall detection supplies S2 while its whole-team facts/plan/progress ledger plus reset/replan authority supplies S3; the same regulator is not S3*.
24|chatdev|Configurable majority-vote execution provides composable S2 conflict resolution; DAG/cycle orchestration remains authored workflow rather than S3.
25|letta|Default compaction-triggered reflection launches a distinct autonomous background reviewer that corrects parent memory/skills, establishing S3* without S2/S3/S4/S5 closure.
26|ailice|IACT subprocessor calls are hierarchical S1 delegation and result integration; no independent coordination, regulation, audit, intelligence or policy function is established.
27|crewai|Crew delegation, optional hierarchical management and planning remain task orchestration; the OSS hallucination guardrail is a no-op, leaving metasystemic functions unowned.
28|openagents|The product makes supervision/review an owner/operator control surface; the former assurance swarm is retired, so topology/evidence machinery does not establish autonomous metasystem ownership.
29|agency-swarm|Directional SendMessage/handoff graphs, shared context, per-agent concurrency, and guardrails support autonomous agent teams, but remain application composition and routine validation rather than S2-S5 organizational closure.
30|concordia|Sequential/simultaneous engines mediate autonomous player actions through shared Game Master scheduling and event resolution as constructor-owned S2; world-causality control and scenario rules are not S3/S5 organizational closure.
31|agentlite|ManagerAgent selects and synchronously delegates to workers; manager naming and fallback are not S2/S3, and no complementary audit channel is supplied.
32|r2r|Research mode exposes an optional dedicated critique/second-opinion model as composable S3*, while retrieval/web sensing remains current-task S1 work rather than S4.
33|openhands|Agent Canvas is an operator control/dispatch surface around coding S1s; backend choice, automations and confirmations do not instantiate autonomous S2-S5.
34|praisonai|An optional hierarchical Manager sees every task status, chooses assignees, re-delegates failures and can stop the team run, exposing composable S3 current regulation without S2 closure.
35|stagehand|One browser S1 autonomously executes multi-step goals through DOM/hybrid/CUA tools; self-healing, callbacks and limits remain operational support rather than a metasystem.
36|swe-agent|One coding S1 owns each repository trajectory, while optional RetryAgent reviewer/chooser loops can score or compare completed attempts and force retry/selection, exposing composable S3*.
37|agent-zero|Persistent subordinate contexts, parallel jobs and review-shaped prompts remain generic parent-child delegation; no dedicated S2-S5 organizational decision right is supplied.
